                            <a name="comment2451"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Rescue Me
</h4>

<p>
Sorry, but this story is all over the place. Characters come out of nowhere, then are juxtaposed with unrelated people in the story. <br />
<br />
Frankly, it makes me wonder: Where were the editors at the Times-Herald-Record? Asleep, maybe? <br />
<br />
Seeing as how it doesn't look like this was written in the heat of deadline, it makes me wonder what the editor's excuse could be.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:tvaldemoro@yahoo.com">Tom Valdemoro</a>
	

 at September 06,2007 19:43
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2452"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Rescue Me
</h4>

<p>
It's also a concern, considering how many aspiring narrativistas are out there. <br />
<br />
Lots of people want to and think they can do it. Few people can. (And, for the record, I have been and sometimes do still fall into both of those categories.)
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:davis_valle@syvnews.com">Davis Valle</a>
	

 at September 06,2007 19:47
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment2454"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: Rescue Me
</h4>

<p>
Our readers hated it too. <br />
The original story was longer and built more in sections. The idea was to introduce characters and the scene before everyone started running and flailing and swimming. I wanted you to meet Maureen as she began her day. Show her as she passed the group of kids so you would remember them when they popped up later and then show Chris positioned down by the water. It was an attempt to put everyone in position and get the context out of the way so as not to slow down the action. I'm not sure it worked even when it was longer. Reading it the next day, I could see how connections that were clear in my mind got lost in the writing and cutting. <br />
This didn't work, but maybe the next one will.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:doyle.m.murphy@gmail.com">Doyle</a>
	

 at September 06,2007 22:54
</div>
